1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,243
The most important lump of metal in the
world has been losing weight.

2
00:00:05,293 --> 00:00:07,782
Or everything else has been gaining it.

3
00:00:07,832 --> 00:00:11,428
For a hundred and thirty years,
nobody could tell which.

4
00:00:11,933 --> 00:00:18,068
Because that lump of metal was the kilogram.
Not a copy of it — the unit itself.

5
00:00:18,118 --> 00:00:23,867
By definition it weighed exactly one
kilogram, and it could never be wrong.

6
00:00:24,367 --> 00:00:27,011
And at some point in the
twentieth century,

7
00:00:27,061 --> 00:00:29,705
it started to disagree with
its own copies.

8
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,550
Not by much. By about the weight
of a fingerprint.

9
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:36,950
But when the object in the vault
defines the unit,

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,254
a disagreement that small is a problem with no
solution — because the cylinder

11
00:00:42,304 --> 00:00:43,342
cannot be wrong.

12
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:47,865
This is the story of how we noticed, and
what we had to do about it.

13
00:00:50,967 --> 00:00:53,415
The kilogram began as a decision.

14
00:00:53,465 --> 00:00:59,699
In 1889, the first General Conference on Weights
and Measures sanctioned a cylinder

15
00:00:59,749 --> 00:01:03,561
of platinum and iridium — ninety
per cent platinum,

16
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:09,693
ten per cent iridium — about thirty-nine
millimetres tall and thirty-nine across.

17
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:12,646
The alloy was chosen for a reason.

18
00:01:12,696 --> 00:01:17,857
Platinum is dense, so the object is small
and its surface area is tiny.

19
00:01:17,907 --> 00:01:22,701
It doesn't rust, doesn't oxidise, and
barely reacts with anything.

20
00:01:22,751 --> 00:01:26,738
Iridium makes it harder, so it
wears less when handled.

21
00:01:27,233 --> 00:01:30,536
It was given a name that
stuck: Le Grand K.

22
00:01:30,586 --> 00:01:36,931
It lived in a safe at the International Bureau
of Weights and Measures, in Sèvres,

23
00:01:36,981 --> 00:01:40,362
under three bell jars, one
inside the other.

24
00:01:40,412 --> 00:01:45,665
Three keys, held by three different
people, were needed to reach it.

25
00:01:46,167 --> 00:01:50,149
Copies were made from the same casting
and distributed to countries,

26
00:01:50,199 --> 00:01:55,011
so that every nation could compare its own
standards with something traceable back

27
00:01:55,061 --> 00:01:55,782
to the vault.

28
00:01:55,633 --> 00:02:00,768
And here is the part that sounds like a
joke: in its entire working life,

29
00:02:00,818 --> 00:02:06,664
the official prototype was taken out and
compared with its copies only a handful of

30
00:02:06,714 --> 00:02:09,363
times. Roughly once every forty years.

31
00:02:09,867 --> 00:02:11,274
The reason was simple.

32
00:02:11,324 --> 00:02:15,379
Every comparison meant handling,
cleaning and exposure to air.

33
00:02:15,429 --> 00:02:19,286
Every measurement risked changing
the thing being measured.

34
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,346
The third periodic verification
ran from 1988 to 1992.

35
00:02:26,396 --> 00:02:28,936
The prototype came out of the safe,

36
00:02:28,986 --> 00:02:33,746
and the copies were brought back to
Sèvres from around the world.

37
00:02:34,233 --> 00:02:39,515
When everything was weighed against everything
else, the numbers didn't line up.

38
00:02:39,565 --> 00:02:44,913
Over roughly a century, the copies had gained
mass relative to the prototype — on

39
00:02:44,963 --> 00:02:47,179
average by about fifty micrograms.

40
00:02:47,667 --> 00:02:51,149
Fifty micrograms is fifty
millionths of a gram.

41
00:02:51,199 --> 00:02:57,086
It is less than a grain of sand, less than a
speck of dust you would never see.

42
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,649
And nobody could say what had
actually happened.

43
00:03:00,699 --> 00:03:04,135
Maybe the copies were gaining mass
from contamination.

44
00:03:04,185 --> 00:03:06,459
Maybe the prototype was losing mass,

45
00:03:06,509 --> 00:03:11,623
shedding material from its surface every time
it was cleaned. Most likely, both.

46
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:17,679
But the logic of the old definition made the
question impossible to answer.

47
00:03:17,729 --> 00:03:22,529
The prototype defined the kilogram, so
its mass was one kilogram,

48
00:03:22,579 --> 00:03:25,514
always — by definition, with zero drift.

49
00:03:25,564 --> 00:03:28,797
If it changed, the kilogram
changed with it,

50
00:03:28,847 --> 00:03:32,752
and the rest of the world quietly
changed underneath.

51
00:03:33,267 --> 00:03:35,548
And the kilogram was not alone.

52
00:03:35,598 --> 00:03:40,736
Under the old system it propped up other
units: the mole, the ampere,

53
00:03:40,786 --> 00:03:44,571
the candela all carried its
definition inside them.

54
00:03:44,621 --> 00:03:50,661
A drifting cylinder in a French vault was a
foundation for a sizeable part of the

55
00:03:50,711 --> 00:03:52,090
measurement system.

56
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:54,029
So the plan changed.

57
00:03:54,079 --> 00:04:00,021
Instead of a better object, physics would use
something that cannot be scratched,

58
00:04:00,071 --> 00:04:03,423
contaminated or dropped: a
constant of nature.

59
00:04:06,533 --> 00:04:10,616
The first route was a machine
called the Kibble balance,

60
00:04:10,666 --> 00:04:13,863
invented by Bryan Kibble in
Britain in 1975.

61
00:04:13,833 --> 00:04:15,390
It works in two modes.

62
00:04:15,440 --> 00:04:21,088
In the first, a mass sits on a pan attached to
a coil inside a magnetic field.

63
00:04:21,138 --> 00:04:27,077
Current flows through the coil until the
magnetic force exactly cancels the weight

64
00:04:27,127 --> 00:04:27,954
of the mass.

65
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:31,091
In the second, the mass is taken away,

66
00:04:31,141 --> 00:04:36,017
and the same coil is moved through the
same field at a measured speed,

67
00:04:36,067 --> 00:04:37,495
generating a voltage.

68
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:42,842
Put the two together, and the strength of the
magnet — the hardest thing in the

69
00:04:42,892 --> 00:04:44,886
machine to measure — cancels out.

70
00:04:44,936 --> 00:04:49,840
What remains links a mass to electrical
measurements: to voltage, to resistance,

71
00:04:49,890 --> 00:04:52,317
and through them to the Planck constant.

72
00:04:52,833 --> 00:04:57,833
The American version, NIST-4, stands two
and a half metres tall in a room

73
00:04:57,883 --> 00:05:03,505
underground and works in vacuum, because even
the buoyancy of air is a disturbance

74
00:05:03,555 --> 00:05:04,473
at this level.

75
00:05:04,967 --> 00:05:09,847
Between 2015 and 2017 it weighed masses
more than ten thousand times.

76
00:05:09,897 --> 00:05:15,419
The result was a value for the Planck constant
with an uncertainty of thirteen

77
00:05:15,469 --> 00:05:16,705
parts per billion.

78
00:05:17,200 --> 00:05:22,300
Thirteen parts per billion is roughly one part
in eighty million — the difference

79
00:05:22,350 --> 00:05:25,542
of a spoonful of water in an
Olympic swimming pool.

80
00:05:28,633 --> 00:05:31,498
The second route took the
opposite approach.

81
00:05:31,548 --> 00:05:35,473
If a kilogram is an amount of matter,
then count the matter.

82
00:05:35,967 --> 00:05:41,406
The Avogadro project grew crystals of
silicon-28 — one isotope,

83
00:05:41,456 --> 00:05:48,464
purified to 99.9995 per cent — and ground them
into spheres of almost exactly one

84
00:05:48,514 --> 00:05:52,995
kilogram, just under ninety-four
millimetres across.

85
00:05:53,500 --> 00:05:56,564
They are among the roundest
objects ever made.

86
00:05:56,614 --> 00:06:01,234
The surface deviates from a perfect
sphere by about fifty nanometres.

87
00:06:01,284 --> 00:06:03,603
Scaled up to the size of the Earth,

88
00:06:03,653 --> 00:06:09,221
the distance from the highest mountain to the
deepest trench would be a few metres.

89
00:06:08,367 --> 00:06:12,024
Because the crystal is pure and its
lattice spacing is known,

90
00:06:12,074 --> 00:06:16,945
measuring the sphere's diameter tells you how
many atoms are inside it — a number

91
00:06:16,995 --> 00:06:20,105
with twenty-odd digits, known
well enough to matter.

92
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:26,783
Two completely different methods, one counting
atoms and one balancing electricity

93
00:06:26,833 --> 00:06:29,823
against gravity, had to agree. They did.

94
00:06:32,933 --> 00:06:36,287
On the sixteenth of November
2018, in Versailles,

95
00:06:36,337 --> 00:06:39,969
the General Conference on Weights
and Measures voted.

96
00:06:40,467 --> 00:06:45,033
The Planck constant was
fixed, by agreement,

97
00:06:45,083 --> 00:06:52,483
at exactly 6.62607015 times ten to the
minus thirty-four joule seconds.

98
00:06:52,533 --> 00:07:01,086
No uncertainty, no further measurement — a
defined value, like the speed of light.

99
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:06,225
On the twentieth of May 2019, the new
definition took effect.

100
00:07:06,275 --> 00:07:10,823
The kilogram stopped being a thing
and became a consequence.

101
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:16,013
Le Grand K is still in its vault,
still under its bell jars.

102
00:07:16,063 --> 00:07:18,299
But it is now just an object.

103
00:07:18,349 --> 00:07:23,659
Its mass is measured like anything else,
and it is allowed to drift.

104
00:07:24,167 --> 00:07:28,962
For a hundred and thirty years, humanity
kept its unit of mass in a safe,

105
00:07:29,012 --> 00:07:32,213
and had no way to ask whether
it was still right.

106
00:07:32,263 --> 00:07:34,802
Now the question can be asked anywhere,

107
00:07:34,852 --> 00:07:40,111
by anyone with the right machine — and the
answer will be the same in a thousand

108
00:07:40,161 --> 00:07:40,509
years.

109
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,823
The last digit finally stopped moving.
